WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.564 --> 00:00:04.709
I grew up in a small town in

00:00:04.709 --> 00:00:05.940
the Midwest.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.022
My older brother had a number of

00:00:08.022 --> 00:00:10.109
neurological difficulties.

00:00:10.109 --> 00:00:12.069
And to watch how he was treated,

00:00:12.069 --> 00:00:13.809
I just from a very young age,

00:00:13.809 --> 00:00:15.915
understood that there were people

00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:17.729
in this world who are born into

00:00:17.729 --> 00:00:19.254
circumstances that make

00:00:19.254 --> 00:00:21.451
life much more difficult for them,

00:00:21.451 --> 00:00:23.011
I also continued to be sort of

00:00:23.011 --> 00:00:24.961
shocked that there were things that

00:00:24.961 --> 00:00:26.261
I was told I couldn't or

00:00:26.261 --> 00:00:28.213
shouldn't do because I was a girl.

00:00:28.213 --> 00:00:30.320
And I think that sort of set the

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:32.596
tone for me to really understand

00:00:32.596 --> 00:00:35.041
deeply from an early age that there

00:00:35.041 --> 00:00:37.403
are people who are disadvantaged

00:00:37.403 --> 00:00:39.610
through no fault of their own.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:40.292
[MUSIC]

00:00:40.292 --> 00:00:42.310
I started in 1978, and

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:44.678
I think at that time maybe 20,

00:00:44.678 --> 00:00:47.065
22% of the class were women.

00:00:47.065 --> 00:00:48.752
It was still unusual for

00:00:48.752 --> 00:00:51.027
me coming from such a baseline of

00:00:51.027 --> 00:00:53.390
understanding about business,

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:55.752
about power, about how the world

00:00:55.752 --> 00:00:58.469
works, I had never understood that.

00:00:58.469 --> 00:00:59.945
And the subtext of the business

00:00:59.945 --> 00:01:01.713
school was, I began to understand.

00:01:01.713 --> 00:01:03.067
It was in the air and

00:01:03.067 --> 00:01:05.442
that was enormously valuable for

00:01:05.442 --> 00:01:07.645
me for whatever I might want to do

00:01:07.645 --> 00:01:08.505
in my life.

00:01:08.505 --> 00:01:11.276
Project Redwood is an organization

00:01:11.276 --> 00:01:13.121
open to Andy GSB alums,

00:01:13.121 --> 00:01:15.430
who are interested in working

00:01:15.430 --> 00:01:17.923
together with other GSB alums on

00:01:17.923 --> 00:01:20.252
the issue of global poverty.

00:01:20.252 --> 00:01:21.877
And it came into being because

00:01:21.877 --> 00:01:23.762
we're all at our 25th reunion,

00:01:23.762 --> 00:01:25.387
which is kind of a big thing and

00:01:25.387 --> 00:01:26.308
it was awesome.

00:01:26.308 --> 00:01:28.334
And there are probably 150 people

00:01:28.334 --> 00:01:29.965
in the room, and you're just

00:01:29.965 --> 00:01:31.729
sitting there based in the sense

00:01:31.729 --> 00:01:33.361
of there is great work that can

00:01:33.361 --> 00:01:35.189
be done, that these individuals

00:01:35.189 --> 00:01:36.971
have stepped forward and done.

00:01:36.971 --> 00:01:38.654
I kept looking around and see if

00:01:38.654 --> 00:01:40.339
somebody's going to stand up and

00:01:40.339 --> 00:01:42.087
say, we gotta do something here

00:01:42.087 --> 00:01:43.270
together as a class.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:43.839
Nobody did.

00:01:43.839 --> 00:01:45.627
So I finally did, I'm still

00:01:45.627 --> 00:01:47.273
the girl in the front row.

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:49.632
And I said, we're 25 years out from

00:01:49.632 --> 00:01:51.845
our career, we have extraordinary

00:01:51.845 --> 00:01:53.910
access to financial resources,

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.470
to power, to contacts.

00:01:55.470 --> 00:01:58.200
What if, what if, we as a class,

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.127
decided that we would band together

00:02:01.127 --> 00:02:03.759
and create some kind of an entity

00:02:03.759 --> 00:02:06.392
that would work to make the world

00:02:06.392 --> 00:02:07.772
a better place?

00:02:07.772 --> 00:02:10.788
And that was the spark that created

00:02:10.788 --> 00:02:12.453
Project Redwood.

00:02:12.453 --> 00:02:19.620
[MUSIC]

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:20.309
We look for

00:02:20.309 --> 00:02:22.377
the best projects where dollars

00:02:22.377 --> 00:02:24.369
are leveraged to help whomever

00:02:24.369 --> 00:02:26.521
lift themselves out of poverty.

00:02:26.521 --> 00:02:28.190
What I've been involved

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:30.497
with is called Hero Women Rising.

00:02:30.497 --> 00:02:33.246
It's in the Democratic Republic of

00:02:33.246 --> 00:02:33.890
Congo.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:34.548
And it was

00:02:34.548 --> 00:02:37.099
founded by an extraordinary woman,

00:02:37.099 --> 00:02:39.074
many years ago, who created

00:02:39.074 --> 00:02:41.626
a school where women, regardless of

00:02:41.626 --> 00:02:43.437
what education level they

00:02:43.437 --> 00:02:45.924
have coming in, can learn a trade.

00:02:45.924 --> 00:02:47.716
These are women who then

00:02:47.716 --> 00:02:48.702
can go out and

00:02:48.702 --> 00:02:51.217
begin to earn money on their own.

00:02:51.217 --> 00:02:53.307
We support farming communities.

00:02:53.307 --> 00:02:55.656
We provide support to about 60

00:02:55.656 --> 00:02:58.179
organizations around the world so

00:02:58.179 --> 00:02:59.052
far today.

00:02:59.052 --> 00:03:00.951
Because we are MBAs,

00:03:00.951 --> 00:03:02.749
we do analyze this.

00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:05.087
And it's pretty clear that we've

00:03:05.087 --> 00:03:07.183
helped almost a million people

00:03:07.183 --> 00:03:09.440
across the globe lift themselves

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:11.464
out of poverty at this point.

00:03:15.863 --> 00:03:17.852
The student body at the GSB is now

00:03:17.852 --> 00:03:20.138
highly attuned to entrepreneurship

00:03:20.138 --> 00:03:21.685
in the social space, and

00:03:21.685 --> 00:03:23.086
doing work to help make

00:03:23.086 --> 00:03:24.722
the world a better place.

00:03:24.722 --> 00:03:26.639
I mean, it's really just part of

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:28.148
the air they breathe now.

00:03:28.148 --> 00:03:29.795
A number of those current students

00:03:29.795 --> 00:03:31.042
have joined us, and boy,

00:03:31.042 --> 00:03:32.245
do we need and want them?

00:03:32.245 --> 00:03:32.986
And besides,

00:03:32.986 --> 00:03:34.654
they're going to be the leaders of

00:03:34.654 --> 00:03:36.507
Project Redwood in 30 years, and

00:03:36.507 --> 00:03:38.001
we needed them to start now.

00:03:38.001 --> 00:03:39.826
I think most of the entrepreneurs

00:03:39.826 --> 00:03:40.673
that we fund and

00:03:40.673 --> 00:03:42.759
the organizations that they create,

00:03:42.759 --> 00:03:44.270
I think those will endure.

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:45.763
But I also think there's always a

00:03:45.763 --> 00:03:47.421
new generation coming up behind it.

00:03:47.421 --> 00:03:48.926
It seems to be a kind of

00:03:48.926 --> 00:03:50.607
basic human instinct.

00:03:50.607 --> 00:03:51.926
Aside from all the ones

00:03:51.926 --> 00:03:53.871
that we know about that aren't so

00:03:53.871 --> 00:03:56.024
admirable, there's also one about

00:03:56.024 --> 00:03:57.830
that human impulse that so many

00:03:57.830 --> 00:03:59.915
people have to make things better,

00:03:59.915 --> 00:04:01.582
not so much for themselves,

00:04:01.582 --> 00:04:02.623
but for the people

00:04:02.623 --> 00:04:04.234
that they see around them.

00:04:04.234 --> 00:04:05.619
That's going to endure.

00:04:05.619 --> 00:04:06.724
That's going to endure.

00:04:06.724 --> 00:04:09.822
[MUSIC]

00:04:09.822 --> 00:04:10.860
I'm Carol Head,

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:12.734
co-chair of Project Redwood.

00:04:12.734 --> 00:04:19.460
[MUSIC]

